---
import GuideLayout from '../../components/GuideLayout.astro';

const title = 'How to Set Up 2FA on Epic Games (Authenticator App)';
const description =
  'Enable authenticator-app 2FA on your Epic Games account. Scan the QR code or enter a setup key, then generate 6-digit TOTP codes in your browser.';
---

<GuideLayout
  serviceName="Epic Games"
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/guides/epic-games/"
  datePublished="2026-11-18"
  dateModified="2026-11-18"
  lastReviewed="2026-11-18"
  intro="Epic Games supports standard authenticator apps for Fortnite, Rocket League, the Epic Games Store and Unreal Engine accounts. Epic’s published instructions describe scanning a QR code; if your screen does not expose a manual key, the screenshot route below gets you the same secret."
  minutes={4}
  params={['6 digits', '30 s period', 'SHA-1']}
  ctaTitle="Protect the account, not just the skins"
  ctaBody="An Epic account can hold purchases, linked console identities, and years of progress — and account theft is common in gaming. Save the setup secret and the 8-digit backup codes in your vault before you queue another match."
  deepDive={{
    heading: "Epic Games specifics",
    blocks: [
      {
        title: '2FA is required for some competitive and creator features',
        body: 'Epic has required two-factor authentication for certain competitive play and creator payouts, and some account actions trigger a verification step. Enabling an authenticator app satisfies those requirements just as SMS does, but without depending on your carrier. Check the current rules in your account’s security page if a tournament or payout asks for 2FA.',
      },
      {
        title: 'One Epic account, many linked platforms',
        body: 'Your Epic account can be linked to PlayStation, Xbox, Nintendo and Steam identities. The second factor protects the Epic login, not those platform accounts — each console network has its own 2FA. If your Fortnite progress matters, enable 2FA on the console account as well so a stolen console login cannot unlink or replace the Epic identity.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Recovery codes are the real insurance',
        body: 'Epic generates 8-digit backup codes when you enable 2FA — treat them like a spare key: store them offline, separate from the device that runs your authenticator. If you also save the setup secret in a password manager, you can regenerate codes anywhere — but a compromised password manager exposes both, so use a strong master password and a second factor on the vault itself.',
      },
    ],
  }}
  steps={[
    {
      title: 'Open your Epic account',
      body: 'Sign in at epicgames.com/account (or through the launcher) and open “Password & Security” from the account menu.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Start authenticator setup',
      body: 'Find “Two-Factor Authentication” and choose the authenticator-app option rather than email or SMS. The exact button wording varies, but it is the app/TOTP choice.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Get the secret from the QR screen',
      body: 'Epic shows a QR code. Scan it with an authenticator app, or enter the setup key manually if an alternate entry option is shown below the code. To use the browser generator, screenshot the QR code and paste the image into the generator — decoding happens locally.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Generate a live code',
      body: 'Paste the key or decoded secret into 2facounter.com and label the entry “Epic Games”. The 6-digit code refreshes every 30 seconds and never leaves your browser.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Confirm and save backup codes',
      body: 'Enter the current code in Epic’s verification field and submit. Epic generates 8-digit backup codes during setup — save them before you leave the screen.',
    },
    {
      title: 'Store both secrets',
      body: 'You can also view or regenerate backup codes later by opening the options menu (three dots) next to “Authenticator App” and choosing “Manage Backup Codes”. Put the setup secret in your password manager and keep the backup codes offline.',
    },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'Is Epic Games 2FA standard TOTP?',
      a: 'Yes. Epic’s authenticator-app method uses standard 6-digit, 30-second TOTP with SHA-1, so any compatible authenticator or this browser generator can produce the code from the same secret.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Do I need 2FA to play Fortnite?',
      a: 'Not for ordinary play. Epic has required 2FA for certain competitive events, creator features, and some account actions, and it strongly recommends it for everyone. Enabling it now avoids a scramble when a specific event or payout requires it.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Will 2FA protect my linked PlayStation or Xbox account?',
      a: 'No. It protects the Epic login only. Each console network has its own authentication settings; enable 2FA there separately if you want the whole chain protected.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What if I lose access to my authenticator?',
      a: 'Use one of the 8-digit backup codes first. If you saved the setup secret, paste it into the generator on another device. Without either, contact Epic support — recovery without a second factor is deliberately slow.',
    },
  ]}
/>
